---

We work over $\mathbb{F}_2$ throughout: $w(p)$ equals the number of nonzero coefficients of $p$ reduced mod 2. Let $q_i(x)=(1+x)^i$.

**Key identities (Freshman's dream in characteristic 2).** For every $s\ge 0$,
$$(1+x)^{2s}=(1+x^2)^s,\qquad (1+x)^{2s+1}=(1+x)(1+x^2)^s. \tag{1}$$

**Weight relations.** Substituting $x^2\mapsto y$ does not change the number of nonzero coefficients, so
$$w\bigl((1+x)^{2s}\bigr)=w\bigl((1+y)^s\bigr)=w(q_s). \tag{2}$$
For odd indices, $(1+x)^{2s+1}=(1+x)(1+x^2)^s$: the coefficient of $x^{2k}$ equals the coefficient of $y^k$ in $(1+y)^s$, and the coefficient of $x^{2k+1}$ equals the same value. Hence each nonzero coefficient of $(1+y)^s$ contributes **two** nonzero coefficients, giving
$$w\bigl((1+x)^{2s+1}\bigr)=2\,w(q_s). \tag{3}$$

---

**Theorem.** *For every finite sequence $0\le i_1<i_2<\cdots<i_n$,
$$w(q_{i_1}+q_{i_2}+\cdots+q_{i_n})\ge w(q_{i_1}).$$*

**Proof by strong induction on $i_1$.**

*Base case $i_1=0$.* Here $q_0=1$ and $w(q_0)=1$. If $n=1$ the claim is trivial. If $n\ge 2$, the coefficient of $x^{i_n}$ in $S:=q_0+q_{i_2}+\cdots+q_{i_n}$ equals $1$ (only $q_{i_n}$ has degree $i_n$, with leading coefficient $1$), so $S\ne 0$ and $w(S)\ge 1=w(q_0)$.

*Inductive step.* Assume the claim holds for all valid sequences whose smallest index is $<i_1$ (with $i_1\ge 1$). The case $n=1$ is trivial, so assume $n\ge 2$. Set $S=q_{i_1}+\cdots+q_{i_n}\in\mathbb F_2[x]$.

Split the indices by parity and define, in $\mathbb F_2[y]$,
$$A(y)=\sum_{\substack{j\,:\,i_j\text{ odd}}}(1+y)^{(i_j-1)/2},\qquad B(y)=\sum_{\substack{j\,:\,i_j\text{ even}}}(1+y)^{i_j/2}.$$
Using (1) on each summand,
$$S(x)=(1+x)\,A(x^2)+B(x^2).$$
Now $(1+x)A(x^2)=A(x^2)+xA(x^2)$: $A(x^2)$ contributes to even powers and $xA(x^2)$ to odd powers, while $B(x^2)$ contributes to even powers. Writing $a_k,b_k$ for the coefficients of $A,B$,
$$\text{coeff of }x^{2k}=a_k+b_k,\qquad \text{coeff of }x^{2k+1}=a_k.$$
Since even and odd powers are disjoint,
$$\boxed{\,w(S)=w(A)+w(A+B)\,}. \tag{4}$$
(Here $w(A+B)$ counts nonzero $a_k+b_k$, i.e. the weight of $A+B\in\mathbb F_2[y]$.)

We also need the **triangle inequality** for Hamming weight over $\mathbb F_2$: $w(P+Q)\ge w(P)-w(Q)$ (since $w(P)=w((P+Q)+Q)\le w(P+Q)+w(Q)$). Applying it with $P=B,Q=A$,
$$w(A+B)\ge w(B)-w(A)\;\Longrightarrow\; w(S)=w(A)+w(A+B)\ge w(B). \tag{5}$$

We now distinguish the parity of $i_1$.

---

**Case 1: $i_1$ is even.** Then $i_1$ belongs to the even subsequence, so $B$ is a sum of $q$'s over the strictly increasing sequence $\tfrac{i_1}{2}<\tfrac{i_{j_2}}{2}<\cdots$ (halves of the even $i_j$'s, strictly increasing because the $i_j$ are). Its smallest index is $\tfrac{i_1}{2}<i_1$, so by the induction hypothesis
$$w(B)\ge w\!\left(q_{i_1/2}\right).$$
By (2), $w(q_{i_1})=w(q_{i_1/2})$. Combining with (5),
$$w(S)\ge w(B)\ge w(q_{i_1/2})=w(q_{i_1}).$$

---

**Case 2: $i_1$ is odd.** Write $m=(i_1-1)/2$. Then $i_1$ belongs to the odd subsequence, so $A$ is a sum of $q$'s over a strictly increasing sequence starting at $m<i_1$. By induction,
$$w(A)\ge w(q_m). \tag{6}$$

For $A+B$: note $A+B=\sum_{j=1}^{n}(1+y)^{\lfloor i_j/2\rfloor}$ over $\mathbb F_2$. The values $\lfloor i_j/2\rfloor$ are non-decreasing; over $\mathbb F_2$ repeated equal terms cancel in pairs, leaving a sum of $q$'s over a **strictly increasing** sequence of those values that occur an odd number of times. The value $\lfloor i_1/2\rfloor=m$ occurs exactly once, because $i_2\ge i_1+1=2m+2$ gives $\lfloor i_2/2\rfloor\ge m+1>m$. Hence $m$ is the smallest index in this reduced sequence, and by induction
$$w(A+B)\ge w(q_m). \tag{7}$$
Finally, by (3) with $i_1=2m+1$,
$$w(q_{i_1})=2\,w(q_m).$$
Adding (6) and (7) via (4):
$$w(S)=w(A)+w(A+B)\ge w(q_m)+w(q_m)=2\,w(q_m)=w(q_{i_1}).$$

---

Both cases give $w(S)\ge w(q_{i_1})$, completing the induction. $\blacksquare$

